<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saqifah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/632">632 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saqifah refers to the meeting held in 632 CE at the Saqifah, a covered gathering place of the Banu Sa'ida in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, where companions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> debated the succession within hours of his death. The gathering resulted in the selection of Abu Bakr as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> treat it as the founding moment of the caliphate and as the origin of the dispute over Ali's claim that later divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia Muslims.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>During Muhammad's lifetime the Muslims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> were divided into two groups: the Muhajirun, who had converted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and migrated to Medina with him, and the Ansar, natives of Medina who had invited him to govern their city. Immediately after his death the Ansar gathered at the courtyard of the Banu Sa'ida clan while his close relatives, Ali among them, prepared his body for burial. The usual view is that the Ansar meant to choose a leader from among themselves; their likely candidate was Sa'd ibn Ubada, a chief of the Banu Khazraj, who was sick that day. Madelung suggests instead that they met to re-establish their control of Medina.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>News of the meeting reached Abu Bakr, Umar and Abu Ubaida, who hurried to the Saqifa. Abu Bakr warned the Ansar that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> would recognise the rule of no one outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, Muhammad's tribe. Habab ibn Mundhir, a veteran of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-badr">Battle of Badr</a>, proposed that the Quraysh and the Ansar each choose their own ruler. In the heated argument that followed, by Umar's own account, he asked Abu Bakr to stretch out his hand and pledged allegiance to him, others followed, and Sa'd ibn Ubada was set upon. Madelung reads the violence as a sign that many of the Ansar at first refused.</p><p>Historians give several reasons why a few of the Muhajirun prevailed: the old rivalry between the Banu Aws and the Banu Khazraj, a split within the Khazraj, whose Bashr ibn Sa'd was among the first to back Abu Bakr, and, for Madelung and Caetani, the timely arrival of the Banu Aslam tribe in numbers that filled the streets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In Madelung's view Abu Bakr's authority was at first precarious. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a> and companions such as Abbas and Zubayr gathered at Ali's house, holding that Ali was the rightful successor. Al-Tabari reports that Umar led an armed crowd there and threatened to burn the house down. Accounts differ on when Ali pledged allegiance: al-Baladhuri says at once, while the Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim place it after the death of his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a>.</p><p>According to Ibn Ishaq and Ibn Hisham, Umar later called the pledge to Abu Bakr a falta, a hasty deal whose evil God had averted. The earliest reports were written down in the second century of the Islamic era or later, when the community was already divided, so historians compare <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia accounts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saqifa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the <b>Saqifah of the Banu Sa’ida</b>, a covered hall in Medina, leading companions debated the succession within hours of the Prophet’s death and acclaimed Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, Muhammad’s close companion and father-in-law, as the first caliph, from the Arabic khalifa, “successor” or “deputy”.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
